WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100 align:start position:0%
 
you  we humble residents of Earth are all too  familiar with destructive weather  tornadoes hurricanes hail the size of  golf balls even bizarre phenomena like  fire tornadoes but compared to much of  the universe we've got it pretty easy in  this video we're going to go on a field  trip to some of the most amazing and  terrifying places in the universe where  death isn't just a possibility it's the  rule let's check out the best places in  the universe to die a horrible death  the first stop on our journey is the  tiny brown dwarf star lumen 16 B of  course size is relative on a cosmic  scale the small star is about the same  size as Jupiter though with considerably  more mass lumen 16 B is located just 6.5  light-years from our own Sun making it  the most convenient location on our list  with our current technology it would  only take us about a hundred fifty  thousand years to get there brown dwarf  stars like lumen 16 B are interesting  because they're not hot enough to burn  with the intensity of a larger star like  our Sun that means they can develop  weather patterns similar to those found  on gas giants like Jupiter for this  thought experiment we're going to assume  we have some technology that allows us  to land on the surface or hover in the  atmosphere of our destinations as we  descend through the patchy clouds of  lumen 16 B will realize that this  hellscape is a metal bands dream  location for an album cover the  temperature reaches a searing 2,000  degrees Celsius and those clouds we pass  through don't shower the star with rain  but rather with molten iron so if death  by white-hot metal rain is your thing  lumen 16 B is the ideal vacation spot  for you our next location is a bit of a  trek sitting a whopping 489 light-years  from Earth at first glance the exoplanet  Corot 7b seems like a decent spot to  establish a human colony it's rocky  about five times the mass of Earth and  just shy of twice the size there are  just a couple tiny problems first and  foremost due to its proximity to its  parent star Corot 7b is probably covered  almost entirely with lava this discovery  prompted the creation of a new category  of exoplanets given the terrifying name  lava ocean planets this hellish world is  tidally locked to its star meaning one  side is permanently facing the star and  the other is facing away that means that  the light side of the planet can reach  temperatures up to 2200 degrees Celsius  while the dark side can plunge as low as  minus 210 to make things worse the  temperature on the bright side is so  intense it can vaporize Rock when this  rock vapor is drawn up into the  atmosphere it condenses back into solid  form and helps the surface with blazing  hot stones if that's still not  inhospitable enough for you kuroh 7b is  regarded to be one of the most  volcanically active exoplanets yet  discovered gravitational tugs from one  of its two sister planets caused rampant  volcanism that far exceeds that of  Jupiter's moon Io  which is riddled with over 400 active  volcanoes so if lumen 16b was too tame  for you consider making Corot 7b your  brutal death of choice the third stop on  our list is probably just upset we gave  it the obnoxiously long name HD 189 733  be for the sake of brevity  it'll be known as HD 18 for the rest of  this video HD 18 is located about 63  light-years away in the constellation  vulpecula a weary traveler could be  forgiven for thinking they had made it  to earth as the planet is a friendly  cobalt blue unfortunately for said  traveler it would be the last mistake  they ever made the weather on HD 18 is  incredible and deadly the temperature on  this tidally locked gas giant can reach  upwards of 1,000 degrees Celsius  not as scorching as our previous  destinations but still hot enough to  kill just about anything the wind whips  around the planet at a rate of 2 km/s or  7 times the speed of sound the brave  among us might say so what it's a little  breezy that perpetual super wind is  dangerous enough on its own but it's  what makes the planet blue that's the  real danger while earth gets its  signature blue look from reflected light  from its oceans HD 18 is blue because  its atmosphere consists largely of  superheated silicate particles as we  learn from Kuro 7b strange particles in  the atmosphere can lead to some exotic  forms of rain and HD 18 is a perfect  example of just how absurd it can get  carry it around the planet at 2  kilometers per second is a raging vortex  of glass shards when the silicate  particles condense and fall towards the  surface the fearsome winds carry the  shards of glass with it  creating a perpetual planetary whirlwind  of razor blades landing on HD 18 is the  way to go if you'd like to be instantly  shred  two pieces on a very pretty blue planet  as we near the end of our trip we find  our fourth destination HD 806 o6b  hereafter referred to as HD 806 this  particular exoplanet has a very hard  existence located about 200 light-years  from Earth  HD 806 has the second most eccentric  orbit of any planet yet discovered this  gas giant spends most of it's 111 day  orbit at a distance from its star that  would place it between Earth and Venus  in our solar system  but over a period of about 6 hours when  it's closest to its star HD 806  experiences a temperature spike from  around 500 Celsius all the way up to  1,200 degrees Celsius this extreme spike  in temperature essentially causes the  atmosphere to ignite triggering a  massive explosion every 111 days the  shockwave generated from this explosion  causes massive planet-wide storms and  wind speeds reaching 5 km/s more than  twice the speed of the glass storm  raging on HD 18:00 if you were present  above the clouds of HD 806 during its 6  our ignition period you would see its  star filling more and more of the sky  increasing in brightness by almost a  factor of 1,000 then if you could  somehow survive the increasing heat  you'd be subjected to tremendous force  and pressure from the shockwave you'd go  out with a bang no list of terrifying  space phenomena would be complete  without the ever-popular black hole  specifically we're going to visit the  supermassive black hole contained within  the hyper luminous quasar s500 one for  +81 hereafter referred to as s5 for  almost 60 years scientists thought the  black hole contained within the m87  system was the largest in existence and  it is massive at 6.5 billion times the  mass of our Sun but it's tiny compared  to s5 the s5 black hole has a mass of 40  billion times that of our Sun this  behemoth has an external horizon of 236  point seven billion kilometers over 37  times the diameter of Pluto's orbit  besides being incomprehensibly huge s5  is also incredibly ancient it's thought  to have formed just 1.6 billion years  after the beginning of the universe  black holes are an incredibly complex  aspect of our natural universe and  there's still much speculation about  exactly what  happens far past their event horizon so  if a mysterious death by  spaghettification inside one of the most  ancient and massive destructive forces  in the universe  sounds like the ideal way to go the s5  black hole is a must visit and that  wraps up our little tour of some of the  most ridiculous places to die in the  universe hopefully you found one that  fits the bill and who knows with the  James Webb telescope nearing launch  there might be even more absurdly  terrifying locations just waiting to be  found if you'd like to learn more about  any of the places mentioned in this  video check out the links in the  description make sure to subscribe if  you haven't already follow second  thought on your favorite social media  and leave a comment down below with your  favorite location from the list or one  that wasn't mentioned you can watch my  other space related videos by clicking  here or watch all my videos by clicking  here thanks for watching and I'll see  you in the next video

